#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-prose-counts -- hand-typed counts in shipped prose, gated against a
# command that re-derives them (T-143; the class is K-1202, K-1205, K-1221).
#
# WHY, from what actually happened rather than from principle. A count typed
# into prose is a claim with no owner: it is true on the day it is written and
# nothing anywhere notices when the thing it counts changes. K-1202 elided
# twelve such counts; K-1205 -- a different wave in the SAME session -- re-typed
# four of them, one in the very file K-1202 had just repaired. K-1221 then found
# one that was not merely unguarded but WRONG: two demos told an operator their
# proof-of-work flow solves four proofs while the run reports one. Nothing in
# this repository reads a numeral in prose, so every one of those was found by a
# human eye, weeks late, and the two waves that fought each other could both
# believe they were right.
#
# TWO HALVES, and only the first is a promise about truth.
#
# (a) DECLARATION. A count may carry a marker naming the command that
#     re-derives it:
#
#       <!-- count: 6 ¦ from: git ls-files 'a/b/create_*.rb.tt' | wc -l -->
#
#     and in a Ruby comment or an environment template, the same payload after
#     the comment character:
#
#       # count: 6 ¦ from: git ls-files 'a/b/create_*.rb.tt' | wc -l
#
#     ONE separator, the broken bar, exactly once; everything after `from:` is
#     the command, verbatim, pipes and all. Every declared command is run from
#     the repository root, its last numeric line compared against the declared
#     count, and a mismatch fails printing BOTH numbers. It CHECKS CONSISTENCY,
#     NEVER TRUTH: a command that measures the wrong thing agrees with a count
#     that means nothing, and this gate will call that green forever.
#
# (b) CENSUS. A whole-file NEWLINE-NORMALISED scan for a cardinal -- word or
#     digit -- standing in front of a countable noun. Newline-normalised because
#     a line-based grep cannot see a WRAPPED sentence, and that is not a
#     hypothetical: two separate repair waves missed a site each for exactly
#     that reason, and one of them was a whole paragraph of a deployment runbook.
#     Lines are joined into paragraphs first, then split into sentences, and
#     every judgement below is made inside ONE SENTENCE.
#
# ATTRIBUTION IS SCOPED TO THE SENTENCE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF K-1222.
# The sweep this guard inherits judged whether a figure was attributed by taking
# a ±320-character window around it and looking for a token that would do the
# attributing. It certified a defective sentence because an unrelated line --
# "Requires python3 + numpy." -- happened to fall inside the window, while that
# sentence's BYTE-IDENTICAL TWIN in the sibling demo failed the same test. One
# defect read as clean and its twin read as defective, on the same criterion, in
# the same wave. A proximity window scores a token's DISTANCE and never whether
# that token is doing the work. So a marker here declares the counts in the
# SENTENCE IT IS WRITTEN IN and in no other -- put it inside the sentence, or on
# the line immediately above it with no blank line between, which is the same
# paragraph and therefore the same sentence once the joining is done. A marker a
# paragraph away is not near enough, however few characters away it is, and the
# self-test plants exactly that case and requires the guard to stay RED.
#
# WHAT IS GATED AND WHAT IS ONLY REPORTED. Four classes are gated -- Markdown,
# environment templates, Rake task descriptions and gemspec
# `summary`/`description` strings -- because they are what a reader of this
# repository, an operator running `rake -T` and a stranger on a RubyGems page
# are shown. Ruby COMMENTS are reported instead, for the reason two paragraphs
# down.
#
# THE SIZE OF EITHER HALF IS NOT WRITTEN HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT (K-1242).
# How many counts each class holds, how long the allow file is and how much of
# it is MEASURED rather than merely excused are printed by EVERY RUN, derived
# from the tree in front of it -- `gated:`, `declarations run:`, the allow-file
# line and `REPORTED, NOT GATED:`. They used to be typed into this paragraph,
# where they drifted twice inside one week, because a numeral in a Ruby comment
# is the exact class this guard REPORTS rather than gates: nothing in the
# repository could ever have caught them. A guard whose own header carries
# hand-typed counts about its own corpus is the defect it exists to find, so the
# numerals are gone and the run is the answer. Do not put them back.
#
# THE GEMSPEC CLASS, AND WHAT ADDING A CLASS ACTUALLY BUYS (T-144). It is the
# cheapest slice of the ungated half and it was chosen for the surface rather
# than for the volume: a gem's `summary`/`description` IS its RubyGems landing
# page, the first and often only page a stranger reads, and K-1217 found a false
# claim on exactly that surface. What T-144 measured when it was added, kept
# here as a DATED RECORD of that wave and not as a live figure: of the cardinal
# tokens inside the published gemspec strings the noun vocabulary reached
# exactly one, `two reserved endpoints` in kiosk-server, since elided; the rest
# were version numbers, HTTP status codes, parameter names (`n=168, k=7`) and
# hash widths (`BLAKE2b-256`), which the digit rules below correctly refuse to
# read as quantities. K-1241 then found the vocabulary one noun short of the
# class it had just added -- `policies`, and `backends` beside it -- so
# kiosk-reputation's «Ships two configurable example policies» was reaching
# NEITHER the gated set NOR the printed broad residue, which needs the plural to
# sit immediately after the cardinal. Both nouns are in the vocabulary now and
# that claim is gated, by a `from:` command rather than an excuse. Its value is
# forward-looking: the next hand-typed count written onto a gem's landing page
# is a red build.
#
# ONE THING THAT DOES NOT WORK THERE, and it changes which repair is available:
# A MARKER CANNOT BE WRITTEN INTO PUBLISHED GEMSPEC TEXT. `summary` and
# `description` are strings, so a `<!-- count: … -->` or a bare `count: … ¦
# from: …` inside them would SHIP to the RubyGems page. The declaration arm is
# therefore unavailable in this class, and the two honest repairs are to elide
# the count (preferred -- the enumeration beside it usually carries the same
# information) or to add an allow entry, with a `from:` command when one exists.
# A `#` comment in the gemspec is reported like any other Ruby comment and does
# NOT declare anything in the published strings, on purpose: it is not what the
# reader of the page sees.
#
# FENCED BLOCKS, AND THE HOLE THE EXCLUSION WAS (K-1606). The census below SKIPS
# fenced blocks, deliberately: their numbers belong to whatever produced them,
# and a guard reading them as prose would ask a document to justify its own
# program output. That exclusion was measured to be a hole.
# kiosk-demo-skooti/firmware/README.md quotes a `make test` run under the words
# "Expected output"; a wave moved figures in the producers and left the
# quotation behind, and the ONE that was caught was caught only because it also
# appears in the PROSE sentence above the fence. The rest sat inside the block,
# where nothing in this repository read them. The block is prose that LOOKS like
# output, so a reader trusts it and no gate reads it.
#
# So a fenced block may OPT IN, with one declaration per figure on the lines
# immediately above its opening fence -- an HTML comment, invisible to a reader
# of the rendered page:
#
#   <!-- fence-count: 85 live-signed vectors ¦ from: <command> -->
#   <!-- fence-count: 0 failed ¦ why: <one-line reason> -->
#
# An opted-in block is then held from both ends at once:
#
#   FC-1  a `from:` key's number must be what its command re-derives
#   FC-2  the key must still occur inside the block -- so a figure that moves in
#         the producer is red whether or not the quotation was updated with it
#   FC-3  COMPLETENESS: every standalone digit run in the block must be carried
#         by some declaration's key. Opting in is all-or-nothing on purpose -- a
#         block where three figures are gated and a dozen are not is the state
#         this arm exists to end, and it reads as coverage.
#
# A block with no declaration is skipped exactly as before, so nothing outside
# the opted-in set changes, and the run prints how many blocks have opted in.
#
# AND IT PRINTS THE DENOMINATOR BESIDE IT, WHICH IS THE HONEST HALF. Opting in
# is a JUDGEMENT this script cannot make: measured over this repository, the
# overwhelming majority of fenced blocks quote a command to type, a config, a
# schema or a diagram, and carry no figure a producer could ever contradict --
# so a gate demanding that every block opt in would be a gate demanding noise.
# What would be dishonest is printing the subjects without the population, so
# every run also says how many blocks NO declaration reaches and how many of
# those carry a standalone digit run. `--self-test` fails when that denominator
# collapses to the numerator, because a corpus this size cannot become fully
# opted in -- a zero there is a broken walker, not a finished job.
#
# STILL UNREACHED, so nobody reads the split as complete: `kiosk.tech/` and the
# private repo, which would need this script run there too (and, for
# `kiosk.tech`, the twin-digest discipline `check-publication-paths` uses).
#
# The comments are reported and not gated, and that is a measurement rather than
# a preference: gating them needs one allow entry per comment -- the run prints
# how many that is -- nearly all of which
# would say the same thing -- that an engineering comment enumerating what it is
# describing ("in two or more demos", "behind three gates") is ordinary English
# and not a claim about a countable population -- and an allow-file that large
# stops being read, which is exactly how a gate becomes a green light. So the
# number is PRINTED on every run instead, beside the count of hits the noun
# vocabulary does not reach at all, and moving a file class from reported to
# gated is one line in the class table plus the allow entries someone actually
# wrote. A guard that is green because its allow-file swallowed everything is
# the failure this split exists to avoid.
#
# A RULE-SET SIZE IS DERIVED FROM THE ENUMERATION IT INTRODUCES (K-1721, K-1722).
# The arm above asks a count to name a command. There is one shape where the
# derivation is already in the file and needs no command at all: a comment that
# says how many rules, arms, passes or classes a script has, standing directly
# above the enumeration of them. That enumeration IS the count, so the cardinal
# beside it is a second copy of a number nothing joins to the first -- and this
# is the class that has actually rotted here. Six guard headers carried a size
# their own enumeration refuted; five were exact when they were written and were
# outgrown by a later rule, and the sixth was byte-identical in both published
# repositories at once because the file is a twin.
#
# So RS-1 compares them. It reads a comment line carrying a cardinal directly in
# front of `rules`/`arms`/`passes`/`classes`/`assertions`/`checks`, requires an
# enumeration of THREE OR MORE distinct ids to begin within eight lines below it
# with nothing but comment between, and fails when the two disagree. An id is a
# prefixed number (`CL-1`, `R8`, `TS-3`), a parenthesised letter or roman
# numeral, or a bare capitalised tag in a column (`SEC`, `PM`, `ABS`, `RN`);
# they are grouped into a BLOCK by kind, so one file's several enumerations do
# not pool into one wrong total.
#
# WHY IT IS SO NARROW, and the number that settled it. A cardinal in front of
# one of those nouns, anywhere in this repository's guards and deploy scripts,
# is roughly eighty comment lines -- nearly all of them correct English about a
# SUB-block ("the two arms read only entries that are NEW", "ON TWO RULES
# ONLY"). Requiring the enumeration to BEGIN below the sentence is what
# separates a total from a mention: over every tracked text file here, that
# conjunction matched exactly one line, and it was the live defect. A wider
# rule would arrive red on correct prose, which is the trade K-1097 measured and
# this header already took once, for source comments, three paragraphs up.
#
# THE ARM IS SCOPED TO NEITHER A FILE CLASS NOR AN EXTENSION, deliberately. It
# runs over every readable tracked text file, and its own scoping is the
# enumeration -- a file with no rule table of three or more ids cannot produce a
# finding, whatever its name. Placing it surfaced a gap that was NOT this arm's:
# `classify` read no extensionless file at all, so the `bin/check-*` guards --
# where this class has lived its whole life, and the densest body of engineering
# comment prose in this repository -- were in no class and every other arm here
# was blind to them. They are read now, by shebang (K-1723).
#
# WHAT IT WOULD NOT CATCH. This list is the header's most useful paragraph, so
# nothing here is overtrusted:
#
#   * A declared command that measures the WRONG THING. Consistency, not truth.
#   * A count with no numeral -- "all of them", "each of the tasks above",
#     "both". Nothing here can see those.
#   * A numeral that is right beside an enumeration whose MEMBERS are wrong:
#     "the four demos -- a, b, c, d" where d is not one. The count is checked;
#     the list beside it is not.
#   * MEASUREMENTS rather than counts -- "~1.3 GiB", "~10 s". Those are a
#     different class needing a different instrument, and pretending this one
#     covers them is how the class above got certified empty.
#   * The countable-noun vocabulary is a hand-kept list and is therefore this
#     guard's main blind spot. To keep the blind spot MEASURED rather than
#     assumed, every run also reports how many cardinal-plus-plural-noun hits
#     the vocabulary does NOT gate, so the residue is a number on every run.
#   * Prose inside string literals other than a Rake `desc`, and files whose
#     extension matches no declared class. Both are counted and printed.
#   * Allow-file rot: a recorded reason that has quietly stopped being true. An
#     entry that matches nothing fails, and an entry may carry its own `from:`
#     command, which is run and compared exactly like an inline marker -- so the
#     entries that CAN be held to a measurement are held to one.
#
# RUNNING DECLARED COMMANDS is running content out of tracked files. That is the
# same trust boundary as this repository's Rakefiles and its other checks, and
# CI already executes all of them; a reader who does not trust this tree should
# not be running its scripts at all.
#
# USAGE
#   bin/check-prose-counts              # check this repository, exit 0/1
#   bin/check-prose-counts --list       # every census hit with its sentence
#   bin/check-prose-counts --self-test  # prove it is red on a plant of every arm
#
# Stdlib only: no bundle, no services, no database, no network.

require "open3"
require "fileutils"
require "rbconfig"
require "tmpdir"

SELF_PATH  = File.realpath(__FILE__)
REPO       = File.dirname(File.dirname(SELF_PATH))
ALLOW_FILE = "bin/prose-counts-allow.txt"

# >>> VOCABULARY -- BEGIN
#
# The cardinals, and the countable nouns a cardinal in front of one makes a
# COUNT rather than ordinary English. Declared here, in one place, because this
# vocabulary is the guard's reach: nothing outside it is gated, and the run
# prints how much it left ungated.
CARDINAL_WORDS = %w[
  one two three four five six seven eight nine ten eleven twelve thirteen fourteen fifteen
  sixteen seventeen eighteen nineteen twenty thirty forty fifty sixty seventy eighty ninety
  hundred thousand
].freeze

WORD_VALUE = {
  "one" => 1, "two" => 2, "three" => 3, "four" => 4, "five" => 5, "six" => 6, "seven" => 7,
  "eight" => 8, "nine" => 9, "ten" => 10, "eleven" => 11, "twelve" => 12, "thirteen" => 13,
  "fourteen" => 14, "fifteen" => 15, "sixteen" => 16, "seventeen" => 17, "eighteen" => 18,
  "nineteen" => 19, "twenty" => 20, "thirty" => 30, "forty" => 40, "fifty" => 50, "sixty" => 60,
  "seventy" => 70, "eighty" => 80, "ninety" => 90, "hundred" => 100, "thousand" => 1000
}.freeze

NOUNS = %w[
  actions apps arguments assertions batteries beats checks columns controllers copies cuts
  demos descriptors documents endpoints entries examples fields files gates gems guards
  backends handlers headings initializers jobs migrations operators pins policies proofs queries repos
  repositories rows scenarios sections sites specs tables tasks templates verbs
].freeze
# <<< VOCABULARY -- END

CARD_ALT = "#{CARDINAL_WORDS.join('|')}"
CARDINAL = /(?:#{CARD_ALT})(?:-(?:#{CARD_ALT}))?|\d{1,6}/i.freeze
CARD_AT  = /\b(#{CARDINAL})\b\+?/i.freeze
# How many words after the cardinal the noun may sit: "four shipped queries" is
# a count, and so is "four of the demos". Written as a WORD WINDOW inside one
# sentence rather than a character window, and the difference is the whole of
# K-1222 -- see the header.
NOUN_WITHIN = 3
# The broader shape the vocabulary does not gate: a cardinal in front of any
# plural-looking word. Reported so the blind spot is a number, not a belief.
BROAD    = /\b(?:#{CARD_ALT})\b\s+([a-z][a-z-]{3,})\b/i.freeze
BROAD_STOP = %w[is was has does its this thus less plus his as us gas bus yes vs].freeze

MARKER = /count:\s*(\d+)\s*¦\s*from:\s*(.+?)\s*(?:-->|\z)/.freeze
# Every HTML comment, delimiters included, lifted OUT of a sentence before the
# adjacency test -- markers are parsed from the raw sentence first, so nothing is
# lost. Two reasons. A marker written between the numeral and the noun it
# declares -- `all seven <!-- … --> demos` -- would otherwise HIDE the count
# instead of declaring it (measured: it happened on the first marker written by
# hand). And a comment is a directive to a machine rather than prose to a
# reader: this repository's demo documents carry `<!-- derived: … -->` blocks
# whose own words were being censused as if a reader saw them. Without that a marker written between the numeral
# and the noun it declares -- `all seven <!-- … --> demos` -- HIDES the count
# instead of declaring it, and the census silently loses a site. Measured while
# seeding this guard: it happened on the first marker written by hand.
MARKER_FULL = /<!--.*?-->/m.freeze
# A marker written after a comment character has no closing delimiter, so it is
# given one while the line boundary is still known. Otherwise the "up to `-->`"
# rule runs to the end of the joined paragraph and swallows whatever follows.
BARE_MARKER = /\Acount:\s*(\d+)\s*¦\s*from:\s*(.+?)\s*\z/.freeze

# >>> FILE CLASSES -- BEGIN
#
# Which tracked files are read, and as what. A file matching no class is out of
# scope and is counted in the run's own output, so "the corpus is what I think
# it is" stays a measurement.
#
#   :prose     Markdown -- what a reader of this repository is shown. GATED.
#              `CLAUDE.md` IS IN THIS CLASS AND USED TO BE OUT OF IT, on the
#              stated ground that it is "removed whole at publication; not
#              shipped content". MEASURED FALSE 2026-09-13 (K-1556): this
#              repository is public and serves its own CLAUDE.md at HTTP 200.
#              Bringing it in surfaced six counts, three of them re-derivable
#              (the demo apps, the operator demos, the gems this repo builds)
#              and now MEASURED from bin/prose-counts-allow.txt rather than
#              invisible. `CHANGELOG.md` stays out, on a reason of its own.
#   :env       An environment template's `#` commentary -- what a deployer reads
#              before switching a knob on, which is the reader K-1217 was about.
#              GATED, and read as comment prose rather than as Markdown.
#   :tasks     A Rake `desc` string -- what `rake -T` prints to an operator, so
#              it is prose the tool itself publishes. GATED.
#   :gemspec   A gemspec's `summary` and `description` STRINGS -- the RubyGems
#              landing page, which is the first and often only page a stranger
#              reads about a gem, and the surface K-1217 found a false count on.
#              GATED (T-144). Its COMMENTS are reported like any other Ruby
#              file's, so the split inside the file is the same split the table
#              makes across files: what the gem PUBLISHES is gated, what its
#              maintainer writes to the next maintainer is reported.
#   :comments  Ruby comment blocks -- commentary for a reader of the source, in
#              which an enumeration in the same breath is ordinary. REPORTED.
def classify(rel, text)
  base = File.basename(rel)
  return nil if base == "CHANGELOG.md"   # append-only: an old entry records the tree AS IT WAS
  return :prose   if rel.end_with?(".md")
  return :env     if base.end_with?(".env.example")
  return :gemspec if rel.end_with?(".gemspec")
  return :code    if rel.end_with?(".rb", ".rake", ".sh")
  # Extensionless executables, by SHEBANG -- the same derivation
  # `audit/check-script-warnings.rb` and `audit/check-guard-corpus-counts.rb`
  # use, and for the same reason: a guard joins this corpus by existing rather
  # than by being remembered. Every `bin/check-*` in this repository is spelled
  # that way, so without this line the whole guard family -- the densest body of
  # engineering comment prose here -- was in NO class at all (K-1723).
  return :code    if !base.include?(".") && text.start_with?("#!")

  nil
end
# <<< FILE CLASSES -- END

# >>> ARM TABLE -- BEGIN
#
# Every failure this gate can report, with the shape that produces it. `plant`
# is what the self-test does to a throwaway repository to make that arm fire, so
# an arm with no way to demonstrate it cannot be added.
ARMS = [
  {
    id: "PC-1",
    what: "a declared count disagrees with the command that is supposed to re-derive it",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, "docs/declared.md"),
                 "The tree carries <!-- count: 9 ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | wc -l --> " \
                 "nine files today.\n")
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-2",
    what: "a declared command failed, or printed nothing that could be read as a number",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, "docs/broken.md"),
                 "The tree carries <!-- count: 3 ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | no-such-command --> " \
                 "three files today.\n")
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-3",
    what: "a marker that does not parse as `count: N ¦ from: <command>`",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, "docs/malformed.md"),
                 "The tree carries <!-- count: three ¦ from: git ls-files --> three files today.\n")
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-4",
    what: "a count in shipped prose that nothing re-derives and nothing has excused",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, "docs/undeclared.md"), "This repository ships four gems.\n")
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-5",
    what: "an allow entry that matched nothing -- the sentence it excused is gone or its count moved",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, ALLOW_FILE),
                 "#{File.read(File.join(dir, ALLOW_FILE))}docs/prose.md ¦ twelve demos ¦ " \
                 "an entry whose sentence does not exist.\n")
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-6",
    reports: "PC-4",
    what: "a marker one sentence away from the count it claims to declare (K-1222: a window " \
          "scores distance, never whether the token is doing the work)",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      File.write(File.join(dir, "docs/window.md"), <<~MD)
        <!-- count: 3 ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | wc -l -->
        This paragraph is about something else entirely and carries no count at all.

        The tree ships three files, and this sentence is well inside three hundred
        characters of the marker above it without being the sentence it declares.
      MD
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-7",
    reports: "PC-4",
    what: "an undeclared count in a gemspec's `summary` -- the one-line string RubyGems shows " \
          "at the top of the gem's page (T-144)",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "fixture.gemspec")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub('shipping two demos', 'shipping five demos'))
    },
  },
  {
    id: "PC-8",
    reports: "PC-4",
    what: "an undeclared count in a gemspec's `description` HEREDOC, wrapped over two lines so " \
          "the join is what finds it and not a line scan (T-144)",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "fixture.gemspec")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub("names three\n    gates", "names nine\n    gates"))
    },
  },
  {
    id: "RS-1",
    what: "a header that states the SIZE of the rule enumeration directly below it, and the " \
          "enumeration says otherwise (K-1721 -- five guard headers outgrown by a later rule, " \
          "and one twin carrying the same defect in both published repositories)",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "bin/fixture-guard")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub("WHAT IT CHECKS. Four arms",
                                           "WHAT IT CHECKS. Three arms"))
    },
  },
  {
    id: "FC-1",
    what: "an opted-in fenced block whose declared figure disagrees with the command that " \
          "re-derives it -- the producer moved and both the block and its declaration stayed",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "docs/fenced.md")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub("3 files ¦", "5 files ¦").sub("3 files scanned", "5 files scanned"))
    },
  },
  {
    id: "FC-2",
    what: "THE K-1606 DEFECT ITSELF: a figure inside a quoted run that the producer moved and " \
          "the quotation did not -- the declaration's key is no longer in the block",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "docs/fenced.md")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub("3 files scanned", "4 files scanned"))
    },
  },
  {
    id: "FC-3",
    what: "a NEW figure appearing inside an opted-in fenced block that no declaration carries -- " \
          "the way the class regrows once a block is gated",
    plant: lambda { |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "docs/fenced.md")
      File.write(path, File.read(path).sub("0 failed ===", "0 failed, 7 skipped ==="))
    },
  },
].freeze
# <<< ARM TABLE -- END

# ── normalisation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def split_sentences(units)
  units.flat_map { |u| u.gsub(/\s+/, " ").strip.split(/(?<=[.!?])\s+(?=[A-Z(«"'`])/) }
       .reject(&:empty?)
end

# Markdown, joined into paragraphs. A blank line, a heading, a list item, a
# table row and a fence all END the unit; everything else is a continuation, so
# a sentence that wraps over four lines is one string by the time it is judged.
# Fenced blocks are skipped: they are program output and command lines, not
# prose, and their numbers belong to whatever produced them. The fence is
# recognised ANYWHERE on the line rather than only at its start, because a
# checklist writes `**Do this:** ``` cmd` on one line and a start-anchored
# detector reads the whole block that follows as prose -- measured, it turned an
# HTTP status inside a command block into "502 demos".
def prose_units(text)
  units = []
  buf   = +""
  fence = nil
  text.lines.map(&:chomp).each do |line|
    if (m = line.match(/(`{3,}|~{3,})/))
      marker = m[1][0]
      if fence.nil? then fence = marker
      elsif fence == marker then fence = nil
      end
      units << buf
      buf = +""
      next
    end
    next if fence

    s = line.sub(/\A\s*>+\s?/, "")
    if s.strip.empty? || s =~ /\A\s{0,3}\#{1,6}\s/ || s =~ /\A\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s/ || s =~ /\A\s*\|/
      units << buf
      buf = +""
      s = s.sub(/\A\s{0,3}\#+\s*/, "").sub(/\A\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s+/, "")
    end
    buf << " " << s
  end
  units << buf
  split_sentences(units)
end

# ── fenced blocks (K-1606) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A declaration line: `<!-- fence-count: <key> ¦ from: <command> -->`, or the
# same with `why:` for a figure nothing here can re-derive. `fence-` is part of
# the word so the marker cannot be confused with the sentence-scoped `count:`
# one, in either direction.
FENCE_DECL = /\A<!--\s*fence-count:\s*(.+?)\s*¦\s*(from|why):\s*(.+?)\s*-->\z/.freeze
# The fence itself, recognised anywhere on the line for the same reason
# prose_units does it.
FENCE_AT   = /(`{3,}|~{3,})/.freeze
# A digit run standing ON ITS OWN. Not part of a hex blob, an identifier, a
# version or a dotted number: this is what keeps a quoted public key, `SK-001`,
# `0x00-0xFF`, `Ed25519` and `v1` out of the completeness census, all of which
# occur in the first block to opt in.
FENCE_DIGIT = /(?<![0-9A-Za-z._-])(\d+)(?![0-9A-Za-z._-])/.freeze

# Every fenced block that has opted in, with its declarations and its body.
def fenced_blocks(text)
  lines  = text.lines.map(&:chomp)
  blocks = []
  i = 0
  while i < lines.length
    m = lines[i].match(FENCE_AT)
    if m.nil?
      i += 1
      next
    end

    marker = m[1][0]
    decls  = []
    j = i - 1
    while j >= 0 && (d = lines[j].strip.match(FENCE_DECL))
      decls.unshift({ key: d[1], kind: d[2], payload: d[3], line: j + 1 })
      j -= 1
    end
    k = i + 1
    while k < lines.length
      c = lines[k].match(FENCE_AT)
      break if c && c[1][0] == marker

      k += 1
    end
    # EVERY block, opted in or not. The ones with no declarations are read by
    # no arm -- they are the census the caller prints, which is the only thing
    # that keeps «3 opted in» from reading as coverage of the whole corpus.
    blocks << { decls: decls, body: lines[(i + 1)...k].join("\n"), line: i + 1 }
    i = k + 1
  end
  blocks
end

# FC-1/FC-2/FC-3 over one file's opted-in blocks. Returns findings; the caller
# owns the stats.
def fence_findings(root, rel, text, stats)
  out = []
  fenced_blocks(text).each do |block|
    # THE CENSUS, counted before the arms and never gated: how many fenced
    # blocks this corpus holds, how many of them no declaration reaches, and
    # how many of THOSE carry a standalone digit run this arm therefore checks
    # for nothing. Reported rather than asserted, because opting a block in is
    # a judgement (most fences quote a command to type, not a run with figures
    # in it) and a gate over that judgement is a gate someone switches off.
    stats[:fence_blocks_corpus] += 1
    if block[:decls].empty?
      stats[:fence_blocks_unopted] += 1
      stats[:fence_blocks_unopted_with_digits] += 1 if block[:body].match?(FENCE_DIGIT)
      next
    end

    stats[:fenced_blocks] += 1
    block[:decls].each do |d|
      stats[:fence_declarations] += 1
      if block[:body].scan(Regexp.new(Regexp.escape(d[:key]), Regexp::IGNORECASE)).empty?
        out << { path: rel, id: "FC-2",
                 what: "line #{d[:line]} declares `#{d[:key]}` but the fenced block below no " \
                       "longer carries that text -- the figure moved and the quotation did not" }
      end
      next unless d[:kind] == "from"

      first = d[:key].split.first.to_s
      unless first =~ /\A(?:\d+|#{CARD_ALT})\z/i
        out << { path: rel, id: "FC-1",
                 what: "line #{d[:line]}: a `from:` declaration's key must BEGIN with the number " \
                       "its command re-derives; this one begins #{first.inspect}" }
        next
      end

      got, why = run_command(root, d[:payload])
      if got.nil?
        out << { path: rel, id: "FC-1",
                 what: "line #{d[:line]}: the declared command #{d[:payload].inspect} #{why}" }
      elsif got != cardinal_value(first)
        out << { path: rel, id: "FC-1",
                 what: "line #{d[:line]}: `#{d[:key]}` but `#{d[:payload]}` says #{got}" }
      else
        stats[:fence_declarations_ok] += 1
      end
    end

    covered = block[:decls].flat_map { |d| d[:key].scan(FENCE_DIGIT).flatten }
    block[:body].scan(FENCE_DIGIT).flatten.uniq.each do |run|
      stats[:fence_figures] += 1
      next if covered.include?(run)

      out << { path: rel, id: "FC-3",
               what: "the figure `#{run}` inside the gated fenced block at line #{block[:line]} is " \
                     "carried by no `fence-count:` declaration above it" }
    end
  end
  out
end

# Ruby: comment runs are joined the same way, and a `desc "…"` is a unit of its
# own. Returned separately because one is gated and the other is reported.
def code_units(text)
  comments = []
  descs    = []
  buf      = +""
  text.lines.map(&:chomp).each do |line|
    if (m = line.match(%r{\A\s*(?:\#+|//+|--)\s?(.*)\z}))
      body = m[1]
      body = "<!-- count: #{Regexp.last_match(1)} ¦ from: #{Regexp.last_match(2)} -->" if body.strip =~ BARE_MARKER
      if body.strip.empty? || body =~ /\A\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s/
        comments << buf
        buf = +""
        body = body.sub(/\A\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s+/, "")
      end
      buf << " " << body
    elsif (m = line.match(/\A\s*desc\s+"([^"]*)"/))
      comments << buf
      buf = +""
      descs << m[1]
    else
      comments << buf
      buf = +""
    end
  end
  comments << buf
  [split_sentences(comments), split_sentences(descs)]
end

# A gemspec, split into what it PUBLISHES and what it merely says (T-144).
#
# The published half is `summary` and `description` -- the two strings RubyGems
# renders as the gem's landing page. Both spellings this repository uses are
# read: a one-line double-quoted string (every `summary` here) and a squiggly
# heredoc (every `description` here), plus adjacent-string continuation, because
# a gemspec that grows a `"..." \` continuation must not silently drop out of
# the corpus. The heredoc BODY is dedented and joined the way Markdown
# paragraphs are, so a sentence wrapped over several lines is one string when it
# is judged -- which is the wrapped-sentence trap K-1202 and K-1217 both fell
# into. (The «five» that stood here was an illustration, not a measurement, and
# it read as one to the K-1531 census; a hypothetical quantity is written in
# words that cannot be mistaken for a count.)
#
# Returns [gated, reported] exactly like `code_units`, and the reported half IS
# `code_units`' comment half: a `#` line in a gemspec is a note to the next
# maintainer and is no more a published claim than one in any other .rb file.
GEMSPEC_FIELD   = /\A\s*\w+\.(?:summary|description)\s*=\s*(.*)\z/.freeze
GEMSPEC_HEREDOC = /\A<<[-~]?["']?([A-Za-z_]+)["']?\s*\z/.freeze
def gemspec_units(text)
  published = []
  lines     = text.lines.map(&:chomp)
  i         = 0
  while i < lines.length
    field = GEMSPEC_FIELD.match(lines[i])
    unless field
      i += 1
      next
    end
    rhs = field[1].strip
    if (heredoc = GEMSPEC_HEREDOC.match(rhs))
      term = heredoc[1]
      body = []
      i += 1
      i += 1 while i < lines.length && lines[i].strip != term && (body << lines[i])
      # Joined into paragraphs on blank lines and list bullets, like prose_units:
      # the unit of judgement is a sentence, never a line.
      buf = +""
      body.each do |line|
        s = line.strip
        if s.empty? || s =~ /\A(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s/
          published << buf
          buf = +""
          s = s.sub(/\A(?:[-*+]|\d+\.)\s+/, "")
        end
        buf << " " << s
      end
      published << buf
    else
      # A quoted string, possibly continued over `\`-joined adjacent literals.
      buf = +""
      loop do
        rhs.scan(/"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"/) { buf << " " << Regexp.last_match(1) }
        break unless rhs.end_with?("\\") && i + 1 < lines.length

        i += 1
        rhs = lines[i].strip
      end
      published << buf
    end
    i += 1
  end
  [split_sentences(published), code_units(text).first]
end

def cardinal_value(token)
  t = token.downcase
  return t.to_i if t =~ /\A\d+\z/

  t.split("-").sum { |w| WORD_VALUE.fetch(w, 0) }
end

# A digit is a count only when it is not part of something else. MEASURED while
# seeding this guard: without these four rules the census reported `K-694` as
# "694 demos", `T-095` as "095 fields", `RFC 9457` as "9457 documents", `0.4` as
# "4 endpoints" and a backticked `400` as "404 beats" -- five false positives in
# one pass, every one of them an identifier, a status code or a version rather
# than a quantity. Cardinal WORDS need none of this; only digits do.
NOT_A_COUNT_BEFORE = %r{(?:[-./`\d–—]|[=:]\s*)\z}.freeze
NAMES_A_NUMBER     = /\b(?:RFC|ADR|HTTP|ISO|SHA|v|K|T|§|status|code|port|version)[\s-]*\z/i.freeze
# What may follow a digit and mean it is not a quantity: a decimal or dotted
# version, a range, a possessive verb ("502'd three demos"), a hyphenated
# compound ("a 2-top"), or a bare parenthesis around it ("`UnknownAction` (404)").
NOT_A_COUNT_AFTER = /\A(?:\.\d|[–—]\s*\d|'|-[A-Za-z])/.freeze

def digit_in_context?(sentence, at, token)
  return false unless token =~ /\A\d+\z/

  pre  = sentence[0, at]
  post = sentence[at + token.length, 3].to_s
  return true if pre =~ NOT_A_COUNT_BEFORE || pre =~ NAMES_A_NUMBER
  return true if post =~ NOT_A_COUNT_AFTER
  return true if pre.end_with?("(") && post.start_with?(")")

  false
end

def hits_in(sentence)
  found = []
  sentence.scan(CARD_AT) do
    m = Regexp.last_match
    next if digit_in_context?(sentence, m.begin(0), m[1])

    words = sentence[m.end(0), 80].to_s.scan(/[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*/).first(NOUN_WITHIN)
    idx   = words.index { |w| NOUNS.include?(w.downcase) }
    next if idx.nil?

    noun = words[idx].downcase
    found << { token: m[1], noun: noun, key: "#{m[1].downcase} #{noun}", value: cardinal_value(m[1]) }
  end
  found
end

def broad_hits_in(sentence)
  n = 0
  sentence.scan(BROAD) do
    w = Regexp.last_match(1).downcase
    n += 1 if w.end_with?("s") && !BROAD_STOP.include?(w) && !NOUNS.include?(w)
  end
  n
end

# ── rule-set size (RS-1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# See the header. The derivation is the enumeration itself, so nothing here runs
# a command; the two halves are a COMMENT LINE naming a size and a BLOCK of ids
# beginning below it.

# An id at the head of a comment line, in a column. Four spellings, because the
# guards in this tree use four: a prefixed number, a bare letter-and-number, a
# parenthesised letter or roman numeral, and a capitalised tag followed by a
# gutter of at least two spaces (`SEC   Every bare section citation`).
RULE_LEAD = /\A\s*(?:\#+|\/\/+|--|\*)/.freeze
RULE_ID   = /\A\s*(?:\#+|\/\/+|--|\*)(?<ind>[ ]{1,20})
              (?<tok>[A-Z]{1,4}-\d{1,3}|[A-Z]\d{1,3}|\([a-z]\)
                    |\((?:i{1,3}|iv|vi{0,3}|ix|xi{0,2})\)
                    |[A-Z]{2,6}(?=[ ]{2,}))
              (?:[ ]+\S|[ ]*\z)/x.freeze
RULE_NOUNS = /(?:arms|rules|passes|classes|assertions|checks)/i.freeze
RULE_PHRASE = /\b(#{CARDINAL})[ ]+(#{RULE_NOUNS})\b/i.freeze
# A demonstrative in front of the cardinal makes it a BACK-reference to part of
# a set ("these two rules"), never a statement of the set's size.
RULE_DEMONSTRATIVE = /\b(?:these|those|their|its|last|first|next|other|remaining|further|more|only)[ ]+\z/i.freeze
RULE_LOOKAHEAD = 8   # lines between the sentence and the enumeration it introduces
RULE_GAP       = 12  # lines between two members of ONE enumeration
RULE_MIN       = 3   # members below which an enumeration is not a rule table

def rule_kind(tok, ind)
  return [:paren, 0]                                if tok.start_with?("(")
  return [tok[/\A[A-Z]{1,4}/].to_sym, 0]            if tok =~ /\A[A-Z]{1,4}-?\d/

  [:tag, ind.length]
end

# Maximal runs of same-kind ids, keyed by the line the run STARTS on.
def rule_blocks(lines)
  marks = []
  lines.each_with_index do |line, i|
    m = RULE_ID.match(line)
    marks << [i, rule_kind(m[:tok], m[:ind]), m[:tok]] if m
  end
  blocks = []
  marks.each do |(i, kind, tok)|
    last = blocks.last
    if last && last[:kind] == kind && i - last[:last] <= RULE_GAP
      last[:last] = i
      last[:members] << tok
    else
      blocks << { kind: kind, start: i, last: i, members: [tok] }
    end
  end
  blocks.select { |b| b[:members].uniq.length >= RULE_MIN }
        .to_h { |b| [b[:start], b] }
end

def rule_set_findings(rel, text, stats)
  lines = text.lines.map(&:chomp)
  starts = rule_blocks(lines)
  stats[:rule_enumerations] += starts.length
  out = []
  lines.each_with_index do |line, i|
    next unless line =~ RULE_LEAD
    next if RULE_ID.match?(line)

    line.to_enum(:scan, RULE_PHRASE).map { Regexp.last_match }.each do |m|
      next if RULE_DEMONSTRATIVE.match?(line[0...m.begin(1)])

      stats[:rule_phrases] += 1
      block = nil
      ((i + 1)..(i + RULE_LOOKAHEAD)).each do |j|
        break if j >= lines.length

        if (b = starts[j])
          block = b
          break
        end
        break if RULE_ID.match?(lines[j])
        break unless lines[j] =~ RULE_LEAD || lines[j].strip.empty?
      end
      next if block.nil?

      members = block[:members].uniq
      said    = cardinal_value(m[1])
      if said == members.length
        stats[:rule_introducers_ok] += 1
      else
        out << { path: rel, id: "RS-1",
                 what: "`#{m[0]}` stands above an enumeration of #{members.length}: " \
                       "#{members.join(', ')}",
                 extra: line.strip }
      end
    end
  end
  out
end

# ── declarations ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def markers_in(sentence)
  sentence.to_enum(:scan, MARKER).map { Regexp.last_match }
          .map { |m| { count: m[1].to_i, command: m[2].strip } }
end

def run_command(root, command)
  out, err, st = Open3.capture3("/bin/sh", "-c", command, chdir: root)
  return [nil, "exited #{st.exitstatus}: #{err.strip.split("\n").first}"] unless st.success?

  line = out.lines.map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?).last
  return [nil, "printed nothing"] if line.nil?
  return [nil, "printed #{line.inspect}, which is not a number"] unless line =~ /\A\d+\z/

  [line.to_i, nil]
end

# ── the allow file ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `<path> ¦ <cardinal> <noun> ¦ [from: <command> ¦] <one-line reason>`
#
# The KEY is the path plus the numeral as written and the noun -- so changing
# the count changes the key, the entry stops matching, and the run goes red from
# both ends at once: an unexcused hit AND a stale entry. That is the property
# that makes this an allow file rather than a mute button.
def load_allow(root)
  path = File.join(root, ALLOW_FILE)
  return [[], []] unless File.file?(path)

  entries = []
  errors  = []
  File.readlines(path, chomp: true).each_with_index do |line, i|
    next if line.strip.empty? || line.strip.start_with?("#")

    parts = line.split("¦").map(&:strip)
    if parts.length < 3
      errors << "#{ALLOW_FILE}:#{i + 1}: expected `path ¦ cardinal noun ¦ reason`"
      next
    end
    file, key = parts[0], parts[1].downcase
    rest = parts[2..]
    command = nil
    if rest.first.to_s.start_with?("from:")
      command = rest.shift.sub(/\Afrom:\s*/, "")
      # A command may itself contain the separator, so anything up to the last
      # field is part of it.
      command = ([command] + rest[0..-2]).join(" ¦ ") if rest.length > 1
      rest = [rest.last]
    end
    reason = rest.join(" ").strip
    if reason.empty?
      errors << "#{ALLOW_FILE}:#{i + 1}: entry carries no reason"
      next
    end
    entries << { line: i + 1, path: file, key: key, command: command, reason: reason, used: 0 }
  end
  [entries, errors]
end

# ── the scan ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def tracked(root)
  out, err, status = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "-z")
  raise "git ls-files failed in #{root}: #{err}" unless status.success?

  out.split("\0").reject(&:empty?)
end

def readable_text(abs)
  bytes = File.binread(abs)
  return nil if bytes.include?("\0")

  text = bytes.force_encoding(Encoding::UTF_8)
  text.valid_encoding? ? text : nil
rescue SystemCallError
  nil
end

def scan(root)
  allow, findings = load_allow(root)
  findings = findings.map { |e| { path: ALLOW_FILE, id: "PC-5", what: e } }

  stats = Hash.new(0)
  listing = []

  tracked(root).each do |rel|
    abs = File.join(root, rel)
    next unless File.file?(abs)

    text = readable_text(abs)
    next if text.nil?

    # RS-1 runs over EVERY readable tracked text file, before the class gate:
    # the guards this class lives in are extensionless and `classify` returns
    # nil for all of them. Its own scoping is the enumeration it needs to find.
    findings.concat(rule_set_findings(rel, text, stats))

    kind = classify(rel, text)
    if kind.nil?
      stats[:out_of_scope] += 1
      next
    end
    stats[:files] += 1

    gated, reported =
      case kind
      when :prose   then [prose_units(text), []]
      when :env     then [code_units(text).first, []]
      when :gemspec then gemspec_units(text)
      else
        c, d = code_units(text)
        [d, c]
      end

    # Per-class tallies, read by the self-test's non-vacuity arm. A file class
    # whose extractor silently returns nothing looks EXACTLY like a class with
    # nothing to say, and exits 0 forever -- the failure this workspace keeps
    # paying for. So the arm asks each class for a subject in the real tree.
    stats[:"files_#{kind}"] += 1
    stats[:gemspec_units] += gated.length if kind == :gemspec
    findings.concat(fence_findings(root, rel, text, stats)) if kind == :prose

    reported.each do |s|
      stats[:reported_hits] += hits_in(s.gsub(MARKER_FULL, " ")).length
    end

    gated.each do |sentence|
      marks = markers_in(sentence)
      stats[:declarations] += marks.length
      stats[:broad_residue] += broad_hits_in(sentence.gsub(MARKER_FULL, " "))

      marks.each do |mark|
        got, why = run_command(root, mark[:command])
        if got.nil?
          findings << { path: rel, id: "PC-2", what: "the declared command #{mark[:command].inspect} " \
                                                    "#{why}", extra: sentence }
        elsif got != mark[:count]
          findings << { path: rel, id: "PC-1",
                        what: "declared #{mark[:count]}, but `#{mark[:command]}` says #{got}",
                        extra: sentence }
        else
          stats[:declarations_ok] += 1
        end
      end

      # A marker that did not parse is a marker that declares nothing, and the
      # count beside it would then be excused by nobody -- but a half-written
      # marker reads to a human as a declaration, so it fails on its own.
      sentence.scan(/(?<!fence-)count:\s*[^¦\n]{0,40}(?:¦|-->)/) do
        frag = Regexp.last_match(0)
        next if marks.any? { |m| frag.include?(m[:count].to_s) }

        findings << { path: rel, id: "PC-3", what: "a marker that does not parse: #{frag.strip}",
                      extra: sentence }
      end

      hits_in(sentence.gsub(MARKER_FULL, " ")).each do |hit|
        stats[:gated_hits] += 1
        state = "undeclared"
        if marks.any? { |m| m[:count] == hit[:value] }
          stats[:excused_declared] += 1
          state = "declared"
        else
          entry = allow.find { |a| a[:path] == rel && a[:key] == hit[:key] }
          if entry
            entry[:used] += 1
            stats[:excused_allowed] += 1
            state = "allowed"
          else
            findings << { path: rel, id: "PC-4",
                          what: "`#{hit[:key]}` is neither declared in its own sentence nor " \
                                "in #{ALLOW_FILE}",
                          extra: sentence }
          end
        end
        listing << [rel, hit[:key], state, sentence]
      end
    end
  end

  allow.each do |entry|
    next unless entry[:command]

    got, why = run_command(root, entry[:command])
    if got.nil?
      findings << { path: ALLOW_FILE, id: "PC-2",
                    what: "line #{entry[:line]}: the declared command #{entry[:command].inspect} #{why}" }
    elsif got != cardinal_value(entry[:key].split.first)
      findings << { path: ALLOW_FILE, id: "PC-1",
                    what: "line #{entry[:line]}: `#{entry[:key]}` but `#{entry[:command]}` says #{got}" }
    else
      stats[:declarations_ok] += 1
    end
  end

  allow.each do |entry|
    next if entry[:used].positive?

    findings << { path: ALLOW_FILE, id: "PC-5",
                  what: "line #{entry[:line]}: `#{entry[:path]} ¦ #{entry[:key]}` matched nothing" }
  end

  { findings: findings, stats: stats, allow: allow, listing: listing }
end

# ── modes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def report(result, quiet:)
  s = result[:stats]
  unless quiet
    puts "check-prose-counts: #{s[:files]} file(s) in a declared class, #{s[:out_of_scope]} out of scope"
    puts "  gated: #{s[:gated_hits]} count(s) -- #{s[:excused_declared]} declared in their own " \
         "sentence, #{s[:excused_allowed]} in #{ALLOW_FILE}"
    puts "  declarations run: #{s[:declarations] + result[:allow].count { |a| a[:command] }} " \
         "(#{s[:declarations_ok]} agreed with their command)"
    # K-1242: this line and the three around it are the guard's description of
    # its own corpus. They live here, derived on every run, rather than in the
    # header, where they were hand-typed numerals in a Ruby comment -- the class
    # this guard reports rather than gates, so nothing could ever catch them
    # drifting, and they twice did.
    puts "  #{ALLOW_FILE}: #{result[:allow].length} entry(ies), " \
         "#{result[:allow].count { |a| a[:command] }} carrying a `from:` command and therefore " \
         "MEASURED rather than excused"
    puts "  fenced blocks opted in: #{s[:fenced_blocks]} carrying #{s[:fence_declarations]} " \
         "declaration(s) over #{s[:fence_figures]} figure(s) " \
         "(#{s[:fence_declarations_ok]} agreed with their command)"
    # Say the denominator out loud. This arm reaches what has opted in and
    # NOTHING else, so a reader who sees only the line above will read a
    # handful of subjects as coverage of every fence in the tree.
    puts "  fenced blocks THIS ARM DOES NOT READ: #{s[:fence_blocks_unopted]} of " \
         "#{s[:fence_blocks_corpus]} in this corpus have no `fence-count:` declaration, " \
         "#{s[:fence_blocks_unopted_with_digits]} of them carrying a standalone digit run"
    puts "  REPORTED, NOT GATED: #{s[:reported_hits]} count(s) in source comments; " \
         "#{s[:broad_residue]} cardinal-plus-plural hit(s) the noun vocabulary does not reach"
    # RS-1's own two halves, derived on every run for the reason the line above
    # it exists: an arm whose detector has stopped seeing either half exits 0
    # forever and reads as coverage.
    puts "  rule-set sizes (RS-1): #{s[:rule_enumerations]} enumeration(s) of " \
         "#{RULE_MIN}+ ids, #{s[:rule_phrases]} comment(s) naming a rule-set size, " \
         "#{s[:rule_introducers_ok]} of them standing above the enumeration they count"
  end

  if result[:findings].empty?
    unless quiet
      puts
      puts "check-prose-counts: OK -- every count in shipped prose is re-derivable or declared " \
           "excused, and every declared command agrees with the number beside it."
      puts "(Consistency, never truth. See the header for the six things this cannot catch.)"
    end
    return 0
  end

  $stdout.flush
  warn ""
  warn "check-prose-counts: FAIL -- #{result[:findings].length} count(s) nothing re-derives."
  result[:findings].sort_by { |f| [f[:path], f[:id]] }.each do |f|
    warn "  #{f[:path]}: [#{f[:id]}] #{f[:what]}"
    warn "      #{f[:extra][0, 160]}" if f[:extra]
  end
  warn ""
  warn "  Repair, in order of preference: elide the count; or declare it with a marker IN ITS OWN"
  warn "  SENTENCE -- <!-- count: N ¦ from: <command> --> ; or add a line to #{ALLOW_FILE} with a"
  warn "  one-line reason, and a `from:` command if one exists."
  warn "  For [RS-1] the repair is the first of those and nothing else: DELETE the cardinal. The"
  warn "  enumeration under it is the derivation, and a second copy of a number is what rots."
  warn "  Inside a FENCED block: <!-- fence-count: <N> <text from the block> ¦ from: <command> -->"
  warn "  on the line above the fence, or the same with `why:` for a figure nothing re-derives."
  1
end

def run_check(quiet: false)
  report(scan(REPO), quiet: quiet)
end

def run_list
  result = scan(REPO)
  result[:listing].each { |rel, key, state, sentence| puts "#{rel}\t#{key}\t#{state}\t#{sentence}" }
  0
end

# ── self-test ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A census whose file set came out empty exits 0 and reads exactly like a clean
# tree, so this proves the guard red on a plant of every arm -- including the
# window plant, which is the one that would pass under the instrument this guard
# replaces -- and then measures the real tree for non-vacuity.

# >>> GREEN FIXTURES -- BEGIN
GREEN = {
  "lib/a.rb" => "# In this demo three gems are wired, which is ordinary commentary.\nA = 1\n",
  "lib/b.rb" => "B = 2\n",
  "lib/c.rb" => "C = 3\n",
  # A count declared in its own sentence, and the same count wrapped over four
  # lines so the join is exercised rather than assumed.
  "docs/prose.md" => <<~MD,
    # A document

    The tree carries <!-- count: 3 ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | wc -l --> three files
    today, and this sentence wraps so that no single line of it holds both the numeral and
    the noun it counts, which is the shape a line-based scan cannot
    see at all.

    ```
    ## Not a heading and five demos in here are not a count.
    ```
  MD
  # A marker written BETWEEN the numeral and the noun it declares. It must still
  # be seen as a count and still be excused by its own marker: a marker that
  # hides a site is worse than no marker, and this is the shape that does it.
  "docs/split.md" => "This tree has three <!-- count: 3 ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | wc -l --> " \
                     "files in it.\n",
  # An opted-in fenced block. Its figures are GATED rather than skipped, which is
  # the whole of the K-1606 arm -- one declared from a command, one excused with
  # a reason, and nothing else inside the block left uncarried.
  "docs/fenced.md" => <<~MD,
    # A quoted run

    Expected output:

    <!-- fence-count: 3 files ¦ from: git ls-files 'lib/*.rb' | wc -l -->
    <!-- fence-count: 0 failed ¦ why: the failure tally of the same line, zero on a run that passes -->
    ```
    === Results: 3 files scanned, 0 failed ===
    ```
  MD
  # THE RS-1 CONTROL. A guard-shaped script whose header states the size of its
  # own rule table and states it CORRECTLY -- four arms, four ids. It must stay
  # green: RS-1's whole claim is that it separates a wrong total from a right
  # one, and a plant with no control measures only that the pattern matches
  # something. It is extensionless on purpose, because that is what the guards
  # carrying this class are, and `classify` returns nil for it -- so this file
  # is read by RS-1, and -- since K-1723 brought the extensionless scripts into
  # `:code` -- REPORTED rather than gated by the census, exactly like a guard.
  "bin/fixture-guard" => <<~GUARD,
    #!/usr/bin/env ruby
    # fixture-guard -- a stand-in for the shape K-1721 was about.
    #
    # WHAT IT CHECKS. Four arms, over the tree this script lives in.
    #
    #   FG-1  the first rule.
    #   FG-2  the second rule.
    #   FG-3  the third rule.
    #   FG-4  the fourth rule, added later -- which is how the class is born.
    #
    # Two of the rules above are bans, and that sentence names a SUBSET rather
    # than a size: it must not be read as a total.
    exit 0
  GUARD
  # An excused count, with the entry that excuses it below.
  "docs/allowed.md" => "The upstream standard defines seven sections, which are not ours to count.\n",
  # A gemspec, carrying a count in BOTH published spellings -- the one-line
  # `summary` string and the `description` heredoc, the second wrapped over two
  # lines so the join is exercised -- and a count in a COMMENT, which must stay
  # reported rather than gated. The two published ones are excused by allow
  # entries, because a marker cannot be written into published gemspec text
  # without shipping the marker to the RubyGems page (see the header).
  "fixture.gemspec" => <<~SPEC,
    # This comment mentions four gems and must NOT be gated.
    Gem::Specification.new do |spec|
      spec.name = "fixture"
      spec.summary = "A fixture gem shipping two demos"
      spec.description = <<~DESC
        The published description names three
        gates, wrapped across a line break.
      DESC
    end
  SPEC
  ALLOW_FILE => "docs/allowed.md ¦ seven sections ¦ someone else's document; nothing here re-derives it.\n" \
                "fixture.gemspec ¦ two demos ¦ the summary's own count, excused so the green tree stays green.\n" \
                "fixture.gemspec ¦ three gates ¦ the description's own count, wrapped over two lines.\n",
}.freeze
# <<< GREEN FIXTURES -- END

def build_fixture(dir)
  FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(dir, "bin"))
  script = File.join(dir, "bin", File.basename(SELF_PATH))
  File.write(script, File.binread(SELF_PATH))
  File.chmod(0o755, script)
  GREEN.each do |rel, body|
    abs = File.join(dir, rel)
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(abs))
    File.write(abs, body)
  end
  _, err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "init", "-q")
  raise "git init failed: #{err}" unless st.success?
end

def stage(dir)
  _, err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "add", "-A")
  raise "git add failed: #{err}" unless st.success?
end

def run_guard_in(dir)
  Open3.capture3(RbConfig.ruby, File.join(dir, "bin", File.basename(SELF_PATH)))
end

def self_test_fixture(failures)
  Dir.mktmpdir("check-prose-counts") do |dir|
    build_fixture(dir)
    stage(dir)

    out, err, st = run_guard_in(dir)
    if st.exitstatus.zero?
      puts "  green tree: exit 0 -- a declared count, a wrapped sentence, an allow entry, a fence -- OK"
    else
      failures << "the green fixture tree should exit 0; it exited #{st.exitstatus}\n#{out}#{err}"
    end
    unless out =~ /rule-set sizes \(RS-1\): [1-9]\d* enumeration\(s\)/ &&
           out =~ /, [1-9]\d* of them standing above the enumeration they count/
      failures << "the green tree did not READ the RS-1 control: `bin/fixture-guard` states its " \
                  "own rule-table size correctly, so the run must report at least one enumeration " \
                  "and at least one introducer AGREEING with it. A control nothing looked at is " \
                  "not a control:\n#{out}"
    end
    unless out.include?("2 declared in their own sentence")
      failures << "the green tree did not excuse BOTH declared counts -- the wrapped one and the " \
                  "one whose marker splits the numeral from its noun -- through the DECLARATION " \
                  "arm:\n#{out}"
    end

    keep = GREEN.keys.to_h { |rel| [rel, File.binread(File.join(dir, rel))] }

    ARMS.each do |arm|
      arm[:plant].call(dir)
      stage(dir)
      out2, err2, st2 = run_guard_in(dir)
      combined = "#{out2}#{err2}"
      if st2.exitstatus.zero?
        failures << "planting #{arm[:id]} did not turn the guard red"
      elsif !combined.include?("[#{arm[:reports] || arm[:id]}]")
        failures << "#{arm[:id]}'s plant fired, but not under #{arm[:reports] || arm[:id]}:\n#{combined}"
      else
        puts "  plant #{arm[:id]}: exit #{st2.exitstatus}, reported by id -- OK"
      end

      Dir.glob(File.join(dir, "**", "*"), File::FNM_DOTMATCH).each do |abs|
        next unless File.file?(abs)

        rel = abs.delete_prefix("#{dir}/")
        next if rel.start_with?(".git/", "bin/check-")
        next if keep.key?(rel)

        File.delete(abs)
      end
      keep.each { |rel, body| File.write(File.join(dir, rel), body) }
      stage(dir)
    end

    # The window plant deserves its own assertion rather than only an exit code:
    # what makes it a regression test for K-1222 is that the marker IS within a
    # few hundred characters, so a distance-scoring instrument passes it.
    ARMS.find { |a| a[:id] == "PC-6" }[:plant].call(dir)
    stage(dir)
    out3, err3, _ = run_guard_in(dir)
    body = File.read(File.join(dir, "docs/window.md"))
    gap  = body.index("three files") - body.index("count: 3")
    if gap > 320
      failures << "the window fixture put the marker #{gap} characters away; it must be INSIDE the " \
                  "window the old instrument used, or it proves nothing"
    elsif !"#{out3}#{err3}".include?("[PC-4]")
      failures << "the window fixture did not report the undeclared count:\n#{out3}#{err3}"
    else
      puts "  window fixture: marker #{gap} characters away, one sentence out -- still red -- OK"
    end
    keep.each { |rel, b| File.write(File.join(dir, rel), b) }
    Dir.glob(File.join(dir, "docs", "*")).each { |f| File.delete(f) unless keep.key?(f.delete_prefix("#{dir}/")) }
    stage(dir)

    _, _, st4 = run_guard_in(dir)
    failures << "the fixture tree did not return to exit 0 after the plants" unless st4.exitstatus.zero?
  end
end

def run_self_test
  failures = []
  self_test_fixture(failures)

  result = scan(REPO)
  s = result[:stats]
  failures << "the real tree read #{s[:files]} file(s) -- the corpus is empty or nearly so" if s[:files] < 30
  if s[:gated_hits] < 20
    failures << "the real tree found #{s[:gated_hits]} gated count(s) -- the census is vacuous here"
  end
  total_decl = s[:declarations] + result[:allow].count { |a| a[:command] }
  failures << "the real tree ran #{total_decl} declared command(s) -- the declaration arm is vacuous" if total_decl < 1
  # The fence arm has the same vacuity hazard as every other: with no opted-in
  # block in the tree it exits 0 forever and reads as coverage (K-1606).
  if s[:fenced_blocks].to_i < 1 || s[:fence_declarations_ok].to_i < 1
    failures << "the real tree has #{s[:fenced_blocks].to_i} opted-in fenced block(s) and " \
                "#{s[:fence_declarations_ok].to_i} fence declaration(s) agreeing with a command -- " \
                "the fence arm has no subject here and would exit 0 whatever a block said."
  end
  # And the CENSUS beside it has its own vacuity hazard, the opposite way
  # round: if the walker stops seeing un-opted-in blocks the denominator
  # silently becomes the numerator and the honest line starts reading as full
  # coverage. The corpus is overwhelmingly un-opted-in and will stay that way,
  # so a zero here is a broken walker, never a finished job.
  if s[:fence_blocks_corpus].to_i <= s[:fenced_blocks].to_i
    failures << "the census counted #{s[:fence_blocks_corpus].to_i} fenced block(s) against " \
                "#{s[:fenced_blocks].to_i} opted in -- the walker has stopped seeing the blocks " \
                "no declaration reaches, and the denominator it prints is no longer one."
  end

  # RS-1's two halves, each with the vacuity hazard every other arm here has:
  # a detector that has stopped finding enumerations, or stopped finding the
  # sentences that name their size, exits 0 forever and reads as coverage. Both
  # are asked of the REAL tree, not of the fixture.
  if s[:rule_enumerations].to_i < 5
    failures << "the real tree holds #{s[:rule_enumerations].to_i} rule enumeration(s) of " \
                "#{RULE_MIN}+ ids. RS-1's id reader has gone blind, and the arm would exit 0 " \
                "whatever a guard header said."
  end
  if s[:rule_phrases].to_i < 5
    failures << "the real tree holds #{s[:rule_phrases].to_i} comment(s) naming a rule-set size. " \
                "RS-1's phrase reader has gone blind, and the arm would exit 0 whatever an " \
                "enumeration said."
  end
  puts "  real tree: #{s[:rule_enumerations].to_i} rule enumeration(s), " \
       "#{s[:rule_phrases].to_i} size-naming comment(s), " \
       "#{s[:rule_introducers_ok].to_i} introducer(s) agreeing with what they introduce"

  # Every declared file class must have a subject here. A class whose extractor
  # returns nothing is a class that has been switched off, and it reads as
  # coverage forever -- so it fails instead.
  %i[prose env gemspec code].each do |kind|
    next if s[:"files_#{kind}"].to_i.positive?

    failures << "the real tree read NO :#{kind} file at all. A file class with no subject is a " \
                "class that has been switched off, and it exits 0 forever."
  end
  if s[:gemspec_units].to_i < 20
    failures << "the gemspec class extracted #{s[:gemspec_units].to_i} published sentence(s) from " \
                "#{s[:files_gemspec].to_i} gemspec(s). The `summary`/`description` extractor has " \
                "stopped reading the strings it exists for -- most likely a spelling this repo " \
                "started using (an interpolated string, a constant, a different heredoc form) " \
                "that the two patterns do not match (T-144)."
  end
  puts "  real tree: #{s[:files]} file(s), #{s[:gated_hits]} gated count(s), #{total_decl} declared " \
       "command(s) -- non-vacuous"
  puts "  real tree: #{s[:fenced_blocks].to_i} opted-in fenced block(s), " \
       "#{s[:fence_figures].to_i} figure(s) inside them, " \
       "#{s[:fence_declarations_ok].to_i} re-derived by their own command"
  puts "  real tree: #{s[:fence_blocks_unopted].to_i} of #{s[:fence_blocks_corpus].to_i} fenced " \
       "block(s) opted into nothing (#{s[:fence_blocks_unopted_with_digits].to_i} of those carry a " \
       "standalone digit run) -- the census is the denominator, not a backlog"
  puts "  real tree: every file class has a subject -- prose #{s[:files_prose].to_i}, env " \
       "#{s[:files_env].to_i}, gemspec #{s[:files_gemspec].to_i} (#{s[:gemspec_units].to_i} published " \
       "sentence(s)), code #{s[:files_code].to_i}"

  status = report(result, quiet: true)
  failures << "the guard is not green over this repository (exit #{status})" unless status.zero?
  puts "  real tree: check exits #{status}"

  if failures.empty?
    puts
    puts "check-prose-counts --self-test: OK -- red on a plant of every arm including a marker one " \
         "sentence away and well inside the old proximity window, green on a wrapped declared " \
         "sentence and on a rule-set header that counts its own enumeration correctly, and " \
         "non-vacuous over this repository."
    return 0
  end

  $stdout.flush
  warn ""
  warn "check-prose-counts --self-test: FAIL"
  failures.each { |f| warn "  #{f}" }
  1
end

# ── entry point ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

code =
  case ARGV[0]
  when nil           then run_check
  when "--list"      then run_list
  when "--self-test" then run_self_test
  else
    warn "usage: check-prose-counts [--list|--self-test]"
    2
  end
exit code
